---
import Layout from "@/layouts/Layout.astro";
import PageHeader from "@/components/elements/PageHeader.astro";

const steps = [
	{
		n: "1",
		title: "Model the program as an ITS",
		body: "An integer transition system is a tuple with a finite set of variables, a finite set of locations, an initial location, a terminal location, and a finite set of transitions. Each transition carries a guard and an update. Despite the finiteness of these sets, an ITS usually has infinitely many evaluations — this is what makes the state space infinite.",
	},
	{
		n: "2",
		title: "Build a Büchi automaton for ¬φ",
		body: "For every LTL formula φ one can compute a Büchi automaton whose language corresponds to L(φ). MoAT constructs an automaton ℬ¬φ for the negated formula using existing libraries such as Spot, which generate optimized, smaller transition-based accepting automata.",
	},
	{
		n: "3",
		title: "Take the synchronized product",
		body: "The product 𝒫 ⊗ ℬ¬φ combines each ITS transition with each automaton transition whose atoms are consistent with it. Its locations are pairs of an ITS location and an automaton state. Additional transitions to the terminal location cover the case where no guard of the remaining outgoing transitions applies.",
	},
	{
		n: "4",
		title: "Check fair termination",
		body: "The product is fairly terminating on the fair locations if and only if φ is verified. This is where the termination tools take over: KoAT proves fair termination, LoAT refutes it.",
	},
];
---

<Layout
	title="Approach - MoAT"
	description="How MoAT reduces LTL model checking of infinite state systems to fair termination, and how KoAT and LoAT are adapted to prove and disprove it."
	keywords="fair termination, Büchi automaton, synchronized product, integer transition system, LTL verification"
>
	<main class="site-container">
		<PageHeader
			title="Approach"
			description="MoAT recapitulates the classical reduction of LTL verification to fair termination, and then hands the resulting problem to termination provers."
			tags={["Integer transition systems", "Büchi automata", "Fair termination"]}
		/>

		<div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl pb-8">
			<!-- Pipeline -->
			<section class="mb-16">
				<h2 class="mb-6 font-brand text-3xl tracking-normal">From LTL to fair termination</h2>

				<div class="space-y-4">
					{steps.map((step) => (
						<div class="flex gap-4 rounded-xl border border-primary/10 bg-bg-primary/50 p-5 dark:border-neutral-700 dark:bg-neutral-800/30">
							<span class="mt-0.5 grid h-8 w-8 flex-none place-items-center rounded-lg bg-primary/10 font-mono text-sm font-semibold text-primary dark:bg-primary/20 dark:text-primary-light">
								{step.n}
							</span>
							<div>
								<h3 class="mb-2 text-lg font-semibold text-neutral-800 dark:text-neutral-100">
									{step.title}
								</h3>
								<p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-neutral-600 dark:text-neutral-300">
									{step.body}
								</p>
							</div>
						</div>
					))}
				</div>
			</section>

			<!-- Fair termination -->
			<section class="mb-16">
				<h2 class="mb-5 font-brand text-3xl tracking-normal">Fair termination</h2>

				<p class="mb-5 text-base leading-relaxed text-neutral-700 dark:text-neutral-300">
					For fair termination, only a designated subset of locations or transitions is required to
					be evaluated finitely often. An ITS is called <em>fairly terminating</em> on a set of
					locations if there is no initial run that visits some location of that set infinitely
					often.
				</p>

				<div
					class="mb-5 rounded-xl border-l-4 border-primary/60 bg-bg-secondary/50 p-5 dark:bg-neutral-900/40"
				>
					<p
						class="mb-2 text-[11px] font-semibold uppercase tracking-[0.14em] text-primary dark:text-primary-light"
					>
						Definition · Fair termination
					</p>
					<p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-neutral-700 dark:text-neutral-300">
						A run (ℓ₀, σ₀) → (ℓ₁, σ₁) → … is fairly terminating on a set ℒ<sub>f</sub> ⊆ ℒ if
						| &#123; i ∈ ℕ | ℓ<sub>i</sub> ∈ ℒ<sub>f</sub> &#125; | &lt; ∞. An ITS is fairly
						terminating on ℒ<sub>f</sub> if every initial run is fairly terminating on
						ℒ<sub>f</sub>.
					</p>
				</div>

				<div
					class="rounded-xl border-l-4 border-primary/60 bg-bg-secondary/50 p-5 dark:bg-neutral-900/40"
				>
					<p
						class="mb-2 text-[11px] font-semibold uppercase tracking-[0.14em] text-primary dark:text-primary-light"
					>
						Theorem · From Büchi automata to fair termination
					</p>
					<p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-neutral-700 dark:text-neutral-300">
						Let 𝒫 be an ITS and ℬ<sub>¬φ</sub> be a Büchi automaton such that L(ℬ<sub>¬φ</sub>)
						corresponds to L(¬φ). Then the ITS 𝒫 ⊗ ℬ<sub>¬φ</sub> is fairly terminating on
						ℒ × F if and only if φ is verified.
					</p>
				</div>
			</section>

			<!-- Proving -->
			<section class="mb-16">
				<h2 class="mb-5 font-brand text-3xl tracking-normal">Proving fair termination</h2>

				<p class="mb-5 text-base leading-relaxed text-neutral-700 dark:text-neutral-300">
					An ITS is fairly terminating on a location if and only if none of its incoming
					transitions can be executed infinitely often. KoAT uses a <strong>modular</strong>{" "}
					approach to prove ordinary termination: it infers a termination argument for each
					transition individually. To prove fair termination, one therefore only has to consider
					the result of KoAT on all incoming transitions of fair locations.
				</p>

				<div
					class="mb-5 rounded-xl border-l-4 border-primary/60 bg-bg-secondary/50 p-5 dark:bg-neutral-900/40"
				>
					<p
						class="mb-2 text-[11px] font-semibold uppercase tracking-[0.14em] text-primary dark:text-primary-light"
					>
						Observation · Fair termination via incoming transitions
					</p>
					<p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-neutral-700 dark:text-neutral-300">
						An ITS is fairly terminating on a set of fair locations ℒ<sub>f</sub> if and only if
						all transitions (_, _, _, ℓ) with ℓ ∈ ℒ<sub>f</sub> are terminating.
					</p>
				</div>

				<p class="text-base leading-relaxed text-neutral-700 dark:text-neutral-300">
					So one could prove fair termination by assigning a ranking function to each location such
					that the values are bounded and decreasing on transitions to fair locations, and
					non-increasing on all other transitions. This ensures that there is no run which reaches
					fair locations infinitely often. To infer termination arguments, KoAT uses several
					sophisticated improvements of this technique: multiphase-linear ranking functions, a
					modular approach that only considers sub-programs when computing ranking functions, a
					specific technique for triangular weakly non-linear loops, and control-flow refinement.
				</p>
			</section>

			<!-- Disproving -->
			<section class="mb-16">
				<h2 class="mb-5 font-brand text-3xl tracking-normal">Disproving fair termination</h2>

				<p class="mb-5 text-base leading-relaxed text-neutral-700 dark:text-neutral-300">
					LoAT proves non-termination by searching for reachable non-terminating cycles via an
					acceleration driven clause learning calculus. This calculus is suitable for arbitrary
					forms of ITSs, including ITSs with nested loops.
				</p>

				<p class="mb-5 text-base leading-relaxed text-neutral-700 dark:text-neutral-300">
					A cycle is <em>fairly</em> non-terminating if and only if one of its locations is a fair
					location. Thus, to disprove fair termination, one just has to run LoAT until it finds a
					reachable non-terminating cycle that contains a fair location.
				</p>

				<p class="text-base leading-relaxed text-neutral-700 dark:text-neutral-300">
					The counterexample trace found by LoAT is a trace for the product ITS, so its states are
					pairs of ITS locations and automaton states. To obtain a counterexample trace on the
					level of the original ITS, each of these pairs is projected to the respective original
					ITS location.
				</p>
			</section>

			<div
				class="rounded-xl border border-dashed border-primary/20 bg-bg-secondary/40 p-6 dark:border-neutral-700 dark:bg-neutral-900/40"
			>
				<p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-neutral-600 dark:text-neutral-300">
					The full formal development — including the definition of ITS evaluations, the LTL
					syntax and semantics used here, and the proofs — is given in the paper. See the{" "}
					<a
						href="/publications/"
						class="font-medium text-primary hover:underline dark:text-primary-light"
						>publications</a
					> page, and the <a
						href="/evaluation/"
						class="font-medium text-primary hover:underline dark:text-primary-light"
						>evaluation</a
					> for how the approach performs in practice.
				</p>
			</div>
		</div>
	</main>
</Layout>
